Problem 1b.
The uncertainty in y controls the weight assigned to each point. Changing this by under or overestimating uncertainty causes chi^2 to be explode or go to 0. 

Problem 1c.
Chi^2 = 94.7\, sigma_y = 0.5 for 98 degs of freedom N-2 = 98. This cooresponds to a probability of 57% = 0.57 > 0.1 which means that the linear model is statistically consistent with the data.

Problem 1d.

The linear fit completely fails to match the data. Chi^2 is extremely high and probability is ~0. This must be fit with a second degree polynomial model, perhaps with the least squares method.


